1
00:00:02,002 --> 00:00:03,197
MAN: <i>Previously on</i> Gotham...

2
00:00:04,037 --> 00:00:06,302
FALCONE: <i>Jim, meet
my daughter, Sofia.</i>

3
00:00:06,507 --> 00:00:08,017
Do you ever think about
walking away from it all?

4
00:00:08,041 --> 00:00:09,041
Sometimes.

5
00:00:10,878 --> 00:00:13,871
BRUCE: <i>This drawing depicts
a man being healed by water.</i>

6
00:00:15,148 --> 00:00:16,639
<i>That's Ra's al Ghul.</i>

7
00:00:17,718 --> 00:00:18,718
<i>He wants the knife.</i>

8
00:00:20,087 --> 00:00:22,318
AUCTIONEER: <i>The opening
bid will begin at $50,000.</i>

9
00:00:22,556 --> 00:00:24,184
A hundred thousand dollars.

10
00:00:24,525 --> 00:00:26,357
Two million dollars.

11
00:00:26,426 --> 00:00:27,426
(GAVEL BANGING)

12
00:00:27,528 --> 00:00:29,360
Sold to Mr. Bruce Wayne.

13
00:00:30,964 --> 00:00:31,964
(EXHALES)

14
00:00:32,032 --> 00:00:33,832
I guess you could say
I'm your number one fan?

15
00:00:34,001 --> 00:00:35,993
What's black and
white and red all over?

16
00:00:36,303 --> 00:00:37,828
Zebra, with a gaping hole.

17
00:00:38,138 --> 00:00:39,978
I think something is
wrong with your brain, Ed.

18
00:00:40,874 --> 00:00:42,502
ALVAREZ: <i>Gordon,
you've got a visitor.</i>

19
00:00:42,709 --> 00:00:44,789
- You shouldn't have come back here.
- I'm a Falcone.

20
00:00:45,078 --> 00:00:47,558
You came looking for a gangster.
That's exactly what you found.

21
00:00:47,848 --> 00:00:49,688
(RA'S AL GHUL WHISPERING)
<i>That knife is the key</i>

22
00:00:50,183 --> 00:00:51,207
to everything.

23
00:00:54,121 --> 00:00:55,214
Well, not everything.

24
00:00:59,927 --> 00:01:00,927
(HELICOPTER HOVERING)

25
00:01:05,866 --> 00:01:06,866
NILES: Hmm.

26
00:01:07,367 --> 00:01:08,367
Beautiful.

27
00:01:08,569 --> 00:01:10,003
And you bought this at auction?

28
00:01:10,270 --> 00:01:12,239
BRUCE: Supposedly, it
was the embalming knife

29
00:01:12,339 --> 00:01:14,103
of the first-century
Mesopotamian king.

30
00:01:14,508 --> 00:01:15,508
Balahsi.

31
00:01:15,943 --> 00:01:18,469
And what is it that you
want of me, Mr. Wayne?

32
00:01:18,812 --> 00:01:20,256
Anything you can
tell me, Dr. Winthrop.

33
00:01:20,280 --> 00:01:21,976
I wanna know exactly
what it is I bought.

34
00:01:22,783 --> 00:01:23,893
Perhaps you should've
considered that

35
00:01:23,917 --> 00:01:26,277
before you dropped two million
dollars on it, shouldn't you?

36
00:01:26,620 --> 00:01:27,952
Leave it with me overnight.

37
00:01:28,722 --> 00:01:29,722
(DOOR OPENS)

38
00:01:29,790 --> 00:01:30,790
I'm back.

39
00:01:31,959 --> 00:01:34,087
Man, you really need

40
00:01:34,194 --> 00:01:35,194
to clean that room.

41
00:01:36,029 --> 00:01:37,429
My delightful grandson.

42
00:01:37,698 --> 00:01:39,894
I have access to the very clean

43
00:01:39,967 --> 00:01:42,801
historical records room
at Gotham Central Library

44
00:01:43,403 --> 00:01:45,395
and he uses it for his homework.

45
00:01:45,572 --> 00:01:47,063
Oh, Alex, let me introduce you

46
00:01:47,574 --> 00:01:49,736
to Mr. Bruce Wayne
and his guardian

47
00:01:49,943 --> 00:01:51,912
- Alfred Pennyworth.
- What's up?

48
00:01:53,580 --> 00:01:54,580
Whoa.

49
00:01:54,648 --> 00:01:55,843
Where did that come from?

50
00:01:56,450 --> 00:01:58,646
That cuneiform's
definitely pre-Phoenician.

51
00:01:59,286 --> 00:02:03,747
Alex is an avid student of
history and sometimes assists me.

52
00:02:04,625 --> 00:02:05,625
A word, Master Bruce.

53
00:02:07,227 --> 00:02:09,747
Now it's all very well involving
an old geezer, but a young man?

54
00:02:10,597 --> 00:02:12,108
Just you remember
who's after that knife.

55
00:02:12,132 --> 00:02:13,743
What so important
about it that Ra's al Ghul

56
00:02:13,767 --> 00:02:15,759
wants it so badly?
We need to know.

57
00:02:18,338 --> 00:02:19,338
Dr. Winthrop.

58
00:02:19,973 --> 00:02:20,973
Alex.

59
00:02:22,776 --> 00:02:25,177
I have to ask you to keep
this matter between us.

60
00:02:26,013 --> 00:02:28,209
- Of course.
- Yeah, cool.

61
00:02:29,282 --> 00:02:30,282
I mean...

62
00:02:30,550 --> 00:02:31,984
Yes, Mr. Wayne.

63
00:02:32,886 --> 00:02:33,979
I'll check back tomorrow.

64
00:02:35,022 --> 00:02:36,022
Until then.

65
00:02:41,762 --> 00:02:42,762
No.

66
00:02:43,630 --> 00:02:44,859
(GRUNTS)

67
00:02:44,965 --> 00:02:46,433
Come on, Ed. Come on.

68
00:02:47,601 --> 00:02:48,601
You can do this.

69
00:02:51,004 --> 00:02:55,032
This has to be
the perfect riddle.

70
00:02:55,142 --> 00:02:56,142
No!

71
00:02:57,177 --> 00:02:58,177
(GROANS)

72
00:03:02,416 --> 00:03:03,416
Yes.

73
00:03:04,484 --> 00:03:05,484
Yes.

74
00:03:06,787 --> 00:03:07,787
Yes!

75
00:03:08,455 --> 00:03:09,455
Yes!

76
00:03:11,958 --> 00:03:12,958
Is that you?

77
00:03:13,026 --> 00:03:14,466
(SINISTER INSTRUMENTAL
MUSIC PLAYING)

78
00:03:21,168 --> 00:03:22,168
(GRUNTS)

79
00:03:22,235 --> 00:03:24,670
Tomorrow night, Oswald,

80
00:03:26,173 --> 00:03:27,539
is the night you die.

81
00:03:39,586 --> 00:03:40,586
Alex.

82
00:03:41,254 --> 00:03:42,654
Bring me the Palmerian Codex.

83
00:03:53,967 --> 00:03:54,967
Ah!

84
00:03:55,736 --> 00:03:56,736
What is it?

85
00:04:01,608 --> 00:04:03,600
"He, who rises from the waters,

86
00:04:04,044 --> 00:04:05,535
"whom death shall not touch.

87
00:04:06,279 --> 00:04:07,611
"With this sacred blade,

88
00:04:08,448 --> 00:04:10,713
"his final savage destiny

89
00:04:11,585 --> 00:04:12,609
"shall be fulfilled.

90
00:04:13,186 --> 00:04:14,415
"The earth will quake.

91
00:04:14,921 --> 00:04:16,116
"Blood will flow.

92
00:04:16,957 --> 00:04:18,289
"All tremble

93
00:04:20,127 --> 00:04:21,595
"before the Demon's Head."

94
00:04:22,629 --> 00:04:23,824
What's the Demon's Head?

95
00:04:24,998 --> 00:04:26,159
Not what, my boy.

96
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:28,000
Who.

97
00:04:28,902 --> 00:04:31,064
<i>There is a legend in
the time of this knife,</i>

98
00:04:31,471 --> 00:04:32,996
<i>of a man who could not die.</i>

99
00:04:33,440 --> 00:04:34,999
Or who died, but was reborn.

100
00:04:35,575 --> 00:04:39,535
Have you ever, in your reading,
come across the name Ra's al Ghul?

101
00:04:40,614 --> 00:04:41,614
He was a warlord.

102
00:04:41,982 --> 00:04:44,918
<i>But those guys always had
stories invented about them.</i>

103
00:04:45,418 --> 00:04:46,613
They're just stories.

104
00:04:46,720 --> 00:04:48,985
My boy, people
believe in these stories.

105
00:04:50,457 --> 00:04:51,652
They kill for them.

106
00:04:54,261 --> 00:04:56,196
<i>I think Bruce Wayne has
stumbled across something</i>

107
00:04:56,296 --> 00:04:58,026
<i>much more complex
than he imagined.</i>

108
00:04:58,765 --> 00:05:00,495
And infinitely more dangerous.

109
00:05:00,600 --> 00:05:01,966
(KNOCKING ON DOOR)

110
00:05:04,337 --> 00:05:05,498
No one should be here.

111
00:05:06,807 --> 00:05:09,208
Here, take this into my office.

112
00:05:09,309 --> 00:05:11,301
Stay quiet. Quickly.

113
00:05:18,785 --> 00:05:20,083
Dr. Niles Winthrop.

114
00:05:20,153 --> 00:05:21,644
- What a pleasure.
- Who are you?

115
00:05:23,590 --> 00:05:24,614
The museum is closed.

116
00:05:24,758 --> 00:05:26,317
How did you get past the guard?

117
00:05:26,526 --> 00:05:27,653
My apologies.

118
00:05:28,562 --> 00:05:29,791
But my business is pressing.

119
00:05:30,463 --> 00:05:32,898
"Greek, Vase - second Century."

120
00:05:33,400 --> 00:05:35,528
Oh. (LAUGHS)

121
00:05:35,702 --> 00:05:38,467
This was made by a forger in
Marrakech in the 15th century.

122
00:05:38,805 --> 00:05:42,037
He had some talent, but
told the most obscene jokes.

123
00:05:43,343 --> 00:05:45,778
Tell me who you are,
or I shall call the police.

124
00:05:46,379 --> 00:05:49,440
I am looking for a knife Bruce
Wayne brought you earlier today.

125
00:05:51,484 --> 00:05:52,564
I don't know what you mean.

126
00:05:53,987 --> 00:05:57,321
The best liar I ever met
ran a brothel in Shanghai.

127
00:05:58,225 --> 00:06:00,319
Her quite considerable
gift was that

128
00:06:00,727 --> 00:06:02,287
you knew she was
lying, but didn't care.

129
00:06:02,329 --> 00:06:05,163
You so badly
wanted to believe her.

130
00:06:05,532 --> 00:06:06,532
(MAN SCOFFS)

131
00:06:07,367 --> 00:06:10,531
You, Dr. Winthrop,
are a terrible liar.

132
00:06:13,073 --> 00:06:14,073
Where's the knife?

133
00:06:15,375 --> 00:06:16,375
(ALARM RINGING)

134
00:06:17,377 --> 00:06:18,845
The police, they're coming.

135
00:06:20,080 --> 00:06:21,548
I imagine they are.

136
00:06:22,148 --> 00:06:23,148
(CHUCKLES)

137
00:06:44,004 --> 00:06:45,004
(BREATHING SHAKILY)

138
00:06:54,514 --> 00:06:55,514
(CAMERA SHUTTER CLICKING)

139
00:06:57,584 --> 00:06:59,075
HARPER: End of crime, my ass.

140
00:06:59,152 --> 00:07:00,152
Harper?

141
00:07:00,787 --> 00:07:02,915
- Where's Bullock?
- He asked me to fill in.

142
00:07:02,989 --> 00:07:04,582
He took a sabbatical
for a few days.

143
00:07:06,993 --> 00:07:07,993
You baby-sitting me?

144
00:07:08,561 --> 00:07:09,561
Do you need baby-sitting?

145
00:07:10,630 --> 00:07:12,496
- What have we got?
- Dr. Niles Winthrop.

146
00:07:13,133 --> 00:07:14,328
Curator of antiquities.

147
00:07:15,101 --> 00:07:16,581
Looks like somebody
snapped his neck.

148
00:07:17,037 --> 00:07:18,130
No witnesses, of course.

149
00:07:18,605 --> 00:07:19,868
Fire alarm triggered security.

150
00:07:20,573 --> 00:07:22,474
Maybe the dead guy
pulled it right before the...

151
00:07:23,276 --> 00:07:24,608
Yeah, thanks. Anything else?

152
00:07:25,445 --> 00:07:26,445
Yes, actually.

153
00:07:27,447 --> 00:07:29,643
According to Professor
Von Dead's log book,

154
00:07:29,749 --> 00:07:30,826
the last person to visit him,

155
00:07:30,850 --> 00:07:32,512
I'm talking just
yesterday afternoon...

156
00:07:32,619 --> 00:07:33,848
Bruce Wayne.

157
00:07:34,821 --> 00:07:36,119
It's all right, let him in.

158
00:07:38,959 --> 00:07:39,959
Detective Gordon.

159
00:07:40,427 --> 00:07:41,427
What...

160
00:07:46,833 --> 00:07:48,077
- What happened?
- We were thinking

161
00:07:48,101 --> 00:07:49,399
we would ask you the same thing.

162
00:07:50,470 --> 00:07:52,462
You met with Dr. Winthrop
just yesterday, correct?

163
00:07:55,475 --> 00:07:57,000
I bought an old
knife at auction.

164
00:07:57,310 --> 00:07:59,176
Dr. Winthrop agreed
to examine it for me.

165
00:08:01,314 --> 00:08:02,914
I was coming by to
check on his progress.

166
00:08:03,717 --> 00:08:04,717
When was he killed?

167
00:08:04,818 --> 00:08:07,018
HARPER: According to the
fire alarm, just past midnight.

168
00:08:07,220 --> 00:08:08,980
Whoever did it must
have snuck past security.

169
00:08:10,724 --> 00:08:13,455
His grandson. Alex. He's
my age, a little bit younger.

170
00:08:13,526 --> 00:08:15,486
- Have you talked to him?
- No one's seen any kid.

171
00:08:16,096 --> 00:08:17,096
I'll get on it.

172
00:08:22,535 --> 00:08:23,535
Where's the knife?

173
00:08:25,372 --> 00:08:27,052
Alfred was right. I
shouldn't have left it.

174
00:08:27,207 --> 00:08:28,505
Bruce, slow down.

175
00:08:29,809 --> 00:08:30,809
What's this knife?

176
00:08:31,211 --> 00:08:32,971
You think someone
would actually kill for it?

177
00:08:34,748 --> 00:08:36,876
Detective Gordon, I'll tell
you what you want to know.

178
00:08:37,317 --> 00:08:39,343
But all I ask is that
you let me come along.

179
00:08:39,419 --> 00:08:41,530
If this is because of the
knife, then I'm responsible.

180
00:08:41,554 --> 00:08:44,217
Bruce, a man is dead, a
child is potentially missing.

181
00:08:44,491 --> 00:08:45,891
This is not time
to play detective.

182
00:08:46,659 --> 00:08:48,855
What do you know? What's
so special about this knife?

183
00:08:50,397 --> 00:08:52,025
As far as I know,
it's just an old knife.

184
00:08:53,233 --> 00:08:54,861
But I paid a great deal for it.

185
00:08:54,934 --> 00:08:56,974
And there was another
bidder. A very aggressive one.

186
00:08:57,670 --> 00:08:58,670
Barbara Kean.

187
00:09:00,907 --> 00:09:01,907
Right.

188
00:09:02,942 --> 00:09:05,207
Well, Harper will track
down the grandson.

189
00:09:06,212 --> 00:09:08,408
I'll deal with Barbara.
You go home.

190
00:09:08,681 --> 00:09:09,881
I'll call you when I need you.

191
00:09:21,428 --> 00:09:22,428
(FOOTSTEPS APPROACHING)

192
00:09:23,496 --> 00:09:25,488
You're here. Finally.

193
00:09:26,199 --> 00:09:28,930
I've kept you
waiting. Apologies.

194
00:09:30,437 --> 00:09:31,996
I see my guests have arrived.

195
00:09:32,405 --> 00:09:33,405
Oh, yeah.

196
00:09:34,340 --> 00:09:38,038
Gotta say, they don't do
much for the vibe of the place.

197
00:09:38,378 --> 00:09:41,246
If you had acquired the
knife when I first requested it,

198
00:09:41,414 --> 00:09:42,939
they wouldn't be necessary.

199
00:09:46,419 --> 00:09:48,615
What's so special
about this knife anyway?

200
00:09:49,622 --> 00:09:50,920
Sentimental value?

201
00:09:51,224 --> 00:09:54,160
Only a destiny born
of blood and magic

202
00:09:54,461 --> 00:09:56,453
two thousand
years in the making.

203
00:09:58,064 --> 00:09:59,064
Uh-huh.

204
00:09:59,833 --> 00:10:01,193
Now Dog-Boy here
is gonna fetch...

205
00:10:01,501 --> 00:10:02,501
(SCREECHING)

206
00:10:04,938 --> 00:10:07,567
His name is Anubis.

207
00:10:09,742 --> 00:10:10,742
(LOW GROWLING)

208
00:10:11,311 --> 00:10:12,311
And yes.

209
00:10:15,648 --> 00:10:19,642
Bring me the knife.
And you can kill the boy.

210
00:10:19,919 --> 00:10:20,919
(SCREECHING)

211
00:10:29,095 --> 00:10:30,495
(OMINOUS INSTRUMENTAL
MUSIC PLAYING)

212
00:10:37,003 --> 00:10:38,003
Boss.

213
00:10:38,938 --> 00:10:40,531
Knocking, Victor.
We discussed this.

214
00:10:40,773 --> 00:10:41,773
Uh-huh?

215
00:10:42,609 --> 00:10:43,609
She's here.

216
00:10:45,044 --> 00:10:46,044
Show her in.

217
00:10:49,549 --> 00:10:52,280
Ms. Falcone, welcome to Gotham.

218
00:10:52,619 --> 00:10:54,349
Mr. Cobblepot, it's a
pleasure to meet you.

219
00:10:54,954 --> 00:10:56,013
Call me Oswald.

220
00:10:57,991 --> 00:10:58,991
Please, have a seat.

221
00:10:59,792 --> 00:11:01,032
Thank you for coming to see me.

222
00:11:01,961 --> 00:11:03,657
I learned from my father

223
00:11:03,730 --> 00:11:05,631
when the king of Gotham
summons you, you come.

224
00:11:06,199 --> 00:11:07,292
And how is Carmine?

225
00:11:07,667 --> 00:11:09,158
Can we hope to see
him sometime soon?

226
00:11:11,171 --> 00:11:13,011
Shall we just address
the elephant in the room?

227
00:11:14,040 --> 00:11:15,133
By all means.

228
00:11:15,208 --> 00:11:19,202
My father's illegal businesses
hold no interest for me.

229
00:11:20,013 --> 00:11:22,642
I'm here because the Falcone
family has a number of charities

230
00:11:22,715 --> 00:11:23,808
that need my attention.

231
00:11:23,883 --> 00:11:25,194
And you can only
provide that attention

232
00:11:25,218 --> 00:11:27,483
by being here
personally. In my city.

233
00:11:27,720 --> 00:11:29,552
- I understand your concern...
- Oh, do you?

234
00:11:29,656 --> 00:11:30,656
(CHUCKLES)

235
00:11:32,392 --> 00:11:33,451
But I assure you,

236
00:11:34,794 --> 00:11:35,818
it's unfounded.

237
00:11:37,163 --> 00:11:40,065
You know, your father
taught me many things.

238
00:11:40,700 --> 00:11:44,228
Among them was to
nurture a healthy paranoia.

239
00:11:45,004 --> 00:11:47,235
For example, when I took over,

240
00:11:47,740 --> 00:11:49,936
some of his old capos
went underground.

241
00:11:50,276 --> 00:11:53,110
Now, perhaps they've
vanished for good.

242
00:11:54,681 --> 00:11:56,225
Or perhaps they're
waiting for a Falcone

243
00:11:56,249 --> 00:11:57,547
to return to lead a rebellion.

244
00:11:58,117 --> 00:11:59,117
(SCOFFS)

245
00:11:59,452 --> 00:12:00,452
Who knows?

246
00:12:00,553 --> 00:12:01,851
(LAUGHS MANIACALLY)

247
00:12:01,921 --> 00:12:07,622
But, I hear your father's
voice whispering in my ear,

248
00:12:10,396 --> 00:12:11,396
"Be careful."

249
00:12:15,235 --> 00:12:16,235
Now...

250
00:12:16,936 --> 00:12:19,496
I need to know that you
are not on some fool's errand

251
00:12:19,572 --> 00:12:21,097
to rebuild your father's empire.

252
00:12:21,841 --> 00:12:22,841
Otherwise...

253
00:12:25,111 --> 00:12:26,374
I will stab you.

254
00:12:33,353 --> 00:12:35,549
I was 13 when my
father first sent me away.

255
00:12:37,390 --> 00:12:39,120
He said that Gotham
was too dangerous.

256
00:12:41,127 --> 00:12:42,254
But this is my home.

257
00:12:42,829 --> 00:12:43,922
I wanted to come home.

258
00:12:44,297 --> 00:12:45,458
That's why I'm here.

259
00:12:46,899 --> 00:12:47,899
Is that enough?

260
00:12:53,072 --> 00:12:54,938
Gotham, Mother to us all.

261
00:12:58,278 --> 00:12:59,837
I think we have
nothing to worry about.

262
00:13:00,513 --> 00:13:01,981
Give your father
my best, will you?

263
00:13:09,122 --> 00:13:10,122
("WHITE RABBIT" PLAYING)

264
00:13:15,628 --> 00:13:16,628
(DOOR OPENING)

265
00:13:18,631 --> 00:13:19,631
BARBARA: Jim.

266
00:13:21,100 --> 00:13:22,329
What a lovely surprise.

267
00:13:24,604 --> 00:13:25,604
Barbara.

268
00:13:27,073 --> 00:13:28,073
Heard you popped up.

269
00:13:28,274 --> 00:13:30,800
Seems like you landed
on your feet, as usual.

270
00:13:30,910 --> 00:13:31,910
Hmm.

271
00:13:32,979 --> 00:13:34,242
A woman has to stay busy.

272
00:13:36,115 --> 00:13:37,115
You seem different.

273
00:13:37,850 --> 00:13:38,977
You like this me better?

274
00:13:40,787 --> 00:13:42,449
You recently bid
on a knife at auction.

275
00:13:42,689 --> 00:13:43,689
(SCOFFS)

276
00:13:44,257 --> 00:13:45,452
So much for catching up.

277
00:13:45,525 --> 00:13:48,324
That knife is now missing and
the person who had it is dead.

278
00:13:48,895 --> 00:13:50,864
What were you doing
last night around midnight?

279
00:13:51,631 --> 00:13:52,631
I was here

280
00:13:53,132 --> 00:13:54,156
with witnesses.

281
00:13:55,234 --> 00:13:56,234
We done?

282
00:13:57,070 --> 00:13:58,070
No.

283
00:13:59,539 --> 00:14:00,539
This place.

284
00:14:01,040 --> 00:14:02,633
The money you used
to bid on the knife.

285
00:14:03,476 --> 00:14:05,502
- Who's bankrolling you?
- A client.

286
00:14:06,913 --> 00:14:09,678
And, no, though tempting,
I can't give you the name.

287
00:14:10,817 --> 00:14:11,978
BRUCE: Is it Ra's al Ghul?

288
00:14:15,555 --> 00:14:18,184
If it is Ra's al Ghul, you don't
know who you're dealing with.

289
00:14:19,325 --> 00:14:21,556
I don't know who or
what you're talking about.

290
00:14:23,329 --> 00:14:24,490
Now, should I call my lawyer?

291
00:14:27,300 --> 00:14:28,300
We'll be in touch.

292
00:14:28,801 --> 00:14:29,801
Come here.

293
00:14:30,870 --> 00:14:31,870
(MUSIC RISES IN TEMPO)

294
00:14:38,578 --> 00:14:39,578
You followed me?

295
00:14:40,079 --> 00:14:42,458
After I told you to stay away?
What the hell were you thinking?

296
00:14:42,482 --> 00:14:45,042
I told you I'm responsible. You
should have let me come with you.

297
00:14:45,251 --> 00:14:46,310
It's clear she's lying.

298
00:14:46,386 --> 00:14:48,116
I can help you find
the killer, find Alex.

299
00:14:48,221 --> 00:14:49,348
That's my job!

300
00:14:49,555 --> 00:14:50,555
You wanna help?

301
00:14:51,424 --> 00:14:52,424
Who is Ra's al Ghul?

302
00:14:52,892 --> 00:14:54,417
And why didn't you
mention him before?

303
00:14:55,561 --> 00:14:57,052
I wasn't certain
he was involved.

304
00:14:57,697 --> 00:14:59,791
- The way she talked, I...
- You're lying, again.

305
00:15:02,568 --> 00:15:05,037
Ra's al Ghul is the real
leader of The Court of Owls.

306
00:15:05,104 --> 00:15:06,732
He's the one
responsible for the virus

307
00:15:06,806 --> 00:15:08,240
and having me kidnapped.

308
00:15:08,307 --> 00:15:09,307
(SIGHS IN DISBELIEF)

309
00:15:10,443 --> 00:15:12,443
And you didn't think to
mention any of this before?

310
00:15:15,915 --> 00:15:18,612
Why would he want the knife,
huh? What's so important about it?

311
00:15:19,385 --> 00:15:21,684
I have no clue. That's what
I've been trying to find out.

312
00:15:21,754 --> 00:15:22,754
(CELL PHONE RINGING)

313
00:15:24,924 --> 00:15:26,916
- Yeah?
- HARPER: <i>Gordon, the kid's not at home.</i>

314
00:15:27,026 --> 00:15:28,370
<i>Apparently, Grandad
was looking after him</i>

315
00:15:28,394 --> 00:15:29,671
<i>while the parents
were traveling.</i>

316
00:15:29,695 --> 00:15:31,175
<i>He's not at school,
no one's seen him.</i>

317
00:15:31,230 --> 00:15:32,230
Great.

318
00:15:32,298 --> 00:15:33,709
Listen, I need all
the info you can get

319
00:15:33,733 --> 00:15:35,099
on a man named Ra's al Ghul.

320
00:15:35,768 --> 00:15:36,768
He could be connected.

321
00:15:37,036 --> 00:15:38,561
<i>Sure. But how do you spell...</i>

322
00:15:40,473 --> 00:15:41,600
No one's seen the boy.

323
00:15:41,941 --> 00:15:43,204
He's either hiding

324
00:15:43,276 --> 00:15:45,108
or the man who killed
his grandfather has him.

325
00:15:46,212 --> 00:15:48,412
The police were triggered
by a fire alarm at the museum.

326
00:15:48,614 --> 00:15:49,673
Alex could've pulled it,

327
00:15:50,683 --> 00:15:51,742
taken the knife.

328
00:15:51,951 --> 00:15:53,442
If so, he'd be hiding, scared.

329
00:15:55,321 --> 00:15:56,448
Where could he be? Think.

330
00:15:57,857 --> 00:15:58,977
I only met him for a second.

331
00:15:59,025 --> 00:16:00,960
You want to play detective?
Now is your chance.

332
00:16:01,027 --> 00:16:03,172
What was he wearing? What
did he say? Give me anything.

333
00:16:03,196 --> 00:16:05,495
He had on his school uniform.

334
00:16:06,032 --> 00:16:07,933
He just came from studying at...

335
00:16:10,036 --> 00:16:11,129
Gotham Central Library.

336
00:16:11,204 --> 00:16:13,173
His grandfather had a
room there. Alex has a key.

337
00:16:13,439 --> 00:16:14,439
If he's hiding,

338
00:16:14,507 --> 00:16:16,135
he's made a choice
not to trust the cops.

339
00:16:16,209 --> 00:16:17,837
If I show up, he might run.

340
00:16:18,277 --> 00:16:21,008
He needs to see a face
he knows. Come on.

341
00:16:24,283 --> 00:16:25,342
Ed Nygma sent you?

342
00:16:25,818 --> 00:16:27,252
The Edward Nygma?

343
00:16:27,987 --> 00:16:28,987
(SCOFFS)

344
00:16:29,589 --> 00:16:30,750
Do it again.

345
00:16:30,823 --> 00:16:31,823
(BEAT-BOXING)

346
00:16:33,826 --> 00:16:35,818
(SINGING) <i>My once
friend Oswald, now my foe</i>

347
00:16:36,262 --> 00:16:38,026
<i>It's me, Ed, just so you know</i>

348
00:16:38,364 --> 00:16:40,356
<i>It's time we meet,
I think you'll agree</i>

349
00:16:40,600 --> 00:16:42,694
<i>You'll find me where
the Earth meets the sky</i>

350
00:16:42,869 --> 00:16:44,838
<i>Where water flows
and birds fly by</i>

351
00:16:45,137 --> 00:16:47,003
<i>At dusk we'll meet,
I 'Il see you there</i>

352
00:16:47,306 --> 00:16:49,605
<i>We'll settle things
unless you're scared</i>

353
00:16:53,679 --> 00:16:54,679
(EXHALES)

354
00:16:56,015 --> 00:16:57,015
What the hell was that?

355
00:16:57,583 --> 00:16:58,583
We should torture 'em.

356
00:16:59,218 --> 00:17:00,778
Just get them to tell
us where Nygma is.

357
00:17:00,920 --> 00:17:02,354
No, he just found
us on the street.

358
00:17:02,555 --> 00:17:03,999
Well that's exactly
what you would say.

359
00:17:04,023 --> 00:17:05,023
Leave!

360
00:17:06,859 --> 00:17:09,419
That barmaid who kidnapped
Ed said he lost his step.

361
00:17:09,529 --> 00:17:12,089
Clearly she was mistaken.
He's up to all of his old tricks.

362
00:17:13,266 --> 00:17:14,359
So I'll meet him.

363
00:17:15,268 --> 00:17:17,703
You and your men
will hide nearby.

364
00:17:18,538 --> 00:17:20,837
Attack when I say.
Do not kill Ed though.

365
00:17:22,275 --> 00:17:23,709
I want Victor to
freeze him again.

366
00:17:24,043 --> 00:17:26,069
- Huh?
- Not you, the other Victor.

367
00:17:26,379 --> 00:17:27,379
Oh.

368
00:17:27,480 --> 00:17:28,573
"Earth meets sky,

369
00:17:29,382 --> 00:17:31,010
"water flows, birds fly."

370
00:17:32,818 --> 00:17:34,081
Obviously, he means the pier.

371
00:17:35,254 --> 00:17:36,254
Gather your men.

372
00:17:37,490 --> 00:17:38,685
I want Ed Nygma.

373
00:17:41,327 --> 00:17:42,327
Historical Records room?

374
00:17:42,995 --> 00:17:43,995
In the back.

375
00:17:51,504 --> 00:17:52,504
(DOOR OPENS)

376
00:18:01,213 --> 00:18:02,213
Alex?

377
00:18:02,281 --> 00:18:03,647
It's me, Bruce Wayne.

378
00:18:06,218 --> 00:18:07,379
You can come out, it's safe.

379
00:18:09,155 --> 00:18:11,317
Alex, please, I just
wanna help you.

380
00:18:18,497 --> 00:18:19,497
Alex.

381
00:18:20,166 --> 00:18:21,225
This is Detective Gordon.

382
00:18:21,601 --> 00:18:22,601
You can trust him.

383
00:18:24,337 --> 00:18:26,203
Okay. Okay, all right.

384
00:18:32,812 --> 00:18:33,871
I'm so sorry.

385
00:18:36,549 --> 00:18:38,074
(STUTTERING) That
man, he killed him.

386
00:18:38,551 --> 00:18:39,712
He killed my grandfather.

387
00:18:39,785 --> 00:18:41,219
It was a man? You saw his face?

388
00:18:41,287 --> 00:18:42,812
I only saw him from behind.

389
00:18:43,222 --> 00:18:45,714
It was the knife. Did you
know it was dangerous?

390
00:18:47,493 --> 00:18:50,122
No. I had no idea.

391
00:18:50,963 --> 00:18:52,829
Alex, we need to get
you down to the station.

392
00:18:52,932 --> 00:18:55,128
I can't. That man,
he'll think I saw him.

393
00:18:55,668 --> 00:18:58,103
- He'll come after me.
- I will protect you, I promise.

394
00:18:58,170 --> 00:18:59,798
He'll keep coming
till he gets the knife.

395
00:19:02,308 --> 00:19:03,788
Alex, do you have
the knife with you?

396
00:19:04,110 --> 00:19:05,305
No, I hid it.

397
00:19:05,611 --> 00:19:06,611
(WOMAN SCREAMING)

398
00:19:07,513 --> 00:19:08,713
(PEOPLE SCREAMING IN DISTANCE)

399
00:19:12,518 --> 00:19:14,146
Take Alex and meet
me at the station. Go.

400
00:19:23,696 --> 00:19:24,696
(SCREECHING)

401
00:19:26,165 --> 00:19:27,165
(GRUNTS)

402
00:19:30,770 --> 00:19:31,770
(GRUNTING)

403
00:19:35,508 --> 00:19:36,508
Who sent you?

404
00:19:36,609 --> 00:19:37,689
(SPEAKING ANCIENT LANGUAGE)

405
00:19:38,844 --> 00:19:40,005
Thanks for clearing that up.

406
00:19:40,346 --> 00:19:41,466
(BOTH GRUNTING AND GROANING)

407
00:19:42,381 --> 00:19:43,381
(SCREECHING)

408
00:19:52,858 --> 00:19:53,858
Bruce?

409
00:19:54,126 --> 00:19:55,126
Run!

410
00:20:02,535 --> 00:20:03,535
(BOOKSHELF CREAKING)

411
00:20:20,052 --> 00:20:21,213
- Bruce Wayne call in?
- No.

412
00:20:21,387 --> 00:20:22,719
We released a city-wide BOLO.

413
00:20:23,055 --> 00:20:24,332
What about that name I gave you?

414
00:20:24,356 --> 00:20:27,758
Zip. I tried Richard
Gull, Ralph Ghoul...

415
00:20:28,027 --> 00:20:29,027
Detective Gordon?

416
00:20:32,031 --> 00:20:33,055
Ra's al Ghul.

417
00:20:34,600 --> 00:20:37,900
Minister of Antiquities attached
to the consulate of Nanda Parbat.

418
00:20:40,339 --> 00:20:41,363
Thanks for coming in.

419
00:20:42,575 --> 00:20:44,771
Nanda Parbat. That's
in the Himalayas, right?

420
00:20:45,044 --> 00:20:46,044
Well done, Detective.

421
00:20:46,278 --> 00:20:48,543
Few in your country have
even heard of our tiny nation.

422
00:20:50,716 --> 00:20:52,446
Why don't we speak
in my captain's office.

423
00:20:52,551 --> 00:20:53,678
A bit more private.

424
00:20:53,753 --> 00:20:54,753
Of course.

425
00:20:56,388 --> 00:20:58,108
- I'll be with you in a minute.
- Thank you.

426
00:21:03,062 --> 00:21:04,062
I don't get it.

427
00:21:04,130 --> 00:21:05,807
This is the guy that you're
looking at for murder?

428
00:21:05,831 --> 00:21:06,831
Why does he just walk in?

429
00:21:06,932 --> 00:21:08,643
Barbara must've told him
we were looking for him.

430
00:21:08,667 --> 00:21:10,467
- Yeah, but...
- The two thugs at the library.

431
00:21:10,836 --> 00:21:11,916
If they're working for him,

432
00:21:12,571 --> 00:21:14,665
they would've told him
that we've contacted Alex.

433
00:21:15,508 --> 00:21:17,306
He could think that
Alex gave us the knife.

434
00:21:19,578 --> 00:21:20,578
I can use that.

435
00:21:23,182 --> 00:21:24,182
Call Alfred Pennyworth.

436
00:21:24,683 --> 00:21:26,481
See if he's heard
from Bruce Wayne.

437
00:21:27,253 --> 00:21:28,744
That thing bit me.

438
00:21:30,022 --> 00:21:32,082
- I need to get you to a hospital.
- No. No.

439
00:21:32,158 --> 00:21:33,158
Then the police.

440
00:21:33,292 --> 00:21:35,732
- Detective Gordon...
- No, I can't. I can't, they'll find me.

441
00:21:36,428 --> 00:21:37,953
(SIGHS) I'm sorry.

442
00:21:39,231 --> 00:21:40,231
I'm scared.

443
00:21:41,066 --> 00:21:42,066
(SOBBING QUIETLY)

444
00:21:43,169 --> 00:21:44,296
I don't know how you do it.

445
00:21:45,471 --> 00:21:47,167
You fought those men, and I...

446
00:21:49,175 --> 00:21:50,268
I just froze.

447
00:21:52,411 --> 00:21:54,812
Same as when that
man killed my grandfather.

448
00:21:57,750 --> 00:21:58,750
Fear is normal.

449
00:22:00,953 --> 00:22:03,946
In the alley, when
my parents were killed

450
00:22:05,624 --> 00:22:06,717
I was terrified.

451
00:22:09,628 --> 00:22:10,628
I just stood there.

452
00:22:14,733 --> 00:22:16,361
There's nothing
wrong with being afraid.

453
00:22:16,869 --> 00:22:19,498
Once you realize it's normal
then you can put it in its place.

454
00:22:20,406 --> 00:22:22,375
Then, you can act.

455
00:22:24,577 --> 00:22:25,577
Fight back.

456
00:22:36,288 --> 00:22:37,288
I felt bad for you.

457
00:22:39,191 --> 00:22:40,819
When that happened
to your parents.

458
00:22:42,294 --> 00:22:44,058
You were all the kids
at school talked about.

459
00:22:45,197 --> 00:22:46,197
What did they say?

460
00:22:46,699 --> 00:22:49,328
(EXHALES) That you
live alone in a mansion,

461
00:22:49,969 --> 00:22:50,993
don't go to school,

462
00:22:51,871 --> 00:22:53,169
fly around in jets.

463
00:22:54,006 --> 00:22:55,133
They make you sound weird.

464
00:22:56,475 --> 00:22:58,842
- Maybe I am.
- No.

465
00:22:59,245 --> 00:23:00,577
You're cool.

466
00:23:01,213 --> 00:23:02,213
I'm weird.

467
00:23:04,250 --> 00:23:08,187
And you're a little
weird too, I guess.

468
00:23:08,254 --> 00:23:09,415
But...

469
00:23:11,290 --> 00:23:13,054
You're weirdly cool.

470
00:23:18,464 --> 00:23:19,693
(SIGHS)

471
00:23:22,801 --> 00:23:24,133
I'll take you to the knife.

472
00:23:26,906 --> 00:23:29,102
Can I get you anything? Coffee?

473
00:23:29,775 --> 00:23:31,935
That won't be necessary. I
think you know why I'm here.

474
00:23:32,211 --> 00:23:34,544
- Enlighten me, Minister.
- Barbara Kean,

475
00:23:34,947 --> 00:23:37,678
with whom you spoke, was
acting on behalf of my government

476
00:23:37,750 --> 00:23:39,844
to procure a certain property.

477
00:23:41,253 --> 00:23:44,223
Said property is the cultural
patrimony of my people.

478
00:23:44,957 --> 00:23:46,448
Why not bid on
the knife yourself?

479
00:23:46,558 --> 00:23:48,390
There were competing claims.

480
00:23:48,894 --> 00:23:51,625
And if we bid officially,
alarms may have sound

481
00:23:52,031 --> 00:23:54,933
and so it seemed
better to do it quietly.

482
00:23:56,302 --> 00:23:58,237
But Miss Kean

483
00:23:59,471 --> 00:24:00,471
failed.

484
00:24:01,106 --> 00:24:02,106
And now,

485
00:24:02,541 --> 00:24:04,942
regrettably, I understand
there's been a murder?

486
00:24:05,644 --> 00:24:06,644
Dr. Niles Winthrop.

487
00:24:07,379 --> 00:24:09,139
Curator at the Gotham
Natural History Museum.

488
00:24:09,648 --> 00:24:10,648
That's awful.

489
00:24:12,284 --> 00:24:14,913
But the reason
I'm here, Detective,

490
00:24:15,621 --> 00:24:17,432
is to press my claim on
behalf of my government

491
00:24:17,456 --> 00:24:20,483
and entreat you to
return the knife to me.

492
00:24:20,693 --> 00:24:22,662
You say this knife is
important to your country.

493
00:24:23,462 --> 00:24:24,462
Please explain.

494
00:24:24,530 --> 00:24:25,530
(LIGHT CHUCKLE)

495
00:24:26,198 --> 00:24:29,168
There is an ancient
story amongst my people

496
00:24:29,835 --> 00:24:33,533
of a man who could not die.

497
00:24:35,507 --> 00:24:37,772
In his time, he
committed terrible acts.

498
00:24:38,644 --> 00:24:40,340
Raised a great kingdom.

499
00:24:41,613 --> 00:24:42,613
And then vanished.

500
00:24:43,315 --> 00:24:44,315
What happened to him?

501
00:24:44,984 --> 00:24:46,350
If the legend is to be believed,

502
00:24:46,785 --> 00:24:49,619
he walked away from his throne,
vowing that one day he would return

503
00:24:49,688 --> 00:24:51,657
and complete his destiny,

504
00:24:52,257 --> 00:24:54,522
but that would require
a particular knife.

505
00:24:55,527 --> 00:24:56,654
This knife.

506
00:24:56,962 --> 00:24:58,089
A story.

507
00:24:58,330 --> 00:24:59,330
As I said.

508
00:25:00,199 --> 00:25:01,360
But to my people,

509
00:25:03,035 --> 00:25:04,196
very meaningful.

510
00:25:06,672 --> 00:25:08,664
I hope that satisfies
your curiosity.

511
00:25:09,341 --> 00:25:10,341
Some.

512
00:25:11,810 --> 00:25:12,971
But you see, Minister,

513
00:25:14,646 --> 00:25:15,841
my chief concern

514
00:25:17,816 --> 00:25:20,547
is there's a boy, the
grandson of the curator.

515
00:25:21,253 --> 00:25:22,253
He's being hunted.

516
00:25:24,089 --> 00:25:25,717
Now, perhaps that's
because of the knife.

517
00:25:26,358 --> 00:25:27,358
Or perhaps,

518
00:25:28,260 --> 00:25:29,922
the killer thinks
he saw something.

519
00:25:32,998 --> 00:25:33,998
Did he?

520
00:25:35,601 --> 00:25:36,601
No.

521
00:25:38,437 --> 00:25:40,406
- Then he's safe.
- Yes, but you see,

522
00:25:40,506 --> 00:25:42,532
I would have to
know that for certain

523
00:25:42,641 --> 00:25:44,837
before I would do anything
that might affect the case.

524
00:25:45,411 --> 00:25:46,470
Such as release the knife.

525
00:25:48,213 --> 00:25:50,045
May I see the knife, Detective?

526
00:25:51,917 --> 00:25:54,352
It would so set my mind at ease.

527
00:25:58,590 --> 00:25:59,590
(INDISTINCT CHATTER)

528
00:26:01,894 --> 00:26:03,192
Where the bloody hell is Gordon?

529
00:26:03,262 --> 00:26:04,560
Gordon, incoming!

530
00:26:04,663 --> 00:26:06,495
Gordon, where the hell is Bruce?

531
00:26:07,666 --> 00:26:08,666
You!

532
00:26:08,734 --> 00:26:11,499
- Alfred, back off!
- Wait, wait! Get your hands off me.

533
00:26:12,204 --> 00:26:13,515
You don't know what
you've got in there.

534
00:26:13,539 --> 00:26:15,269
I'm starting to, but
you are not helping.

535
00:26:15,374 --> 00:26:16,740
I need you to calm down.

536
00:26:16,809 --> 00:26:18,471
- Let me finish talking to him.
- Gordon.

537
00:26:18,944 --> 00:26:20,242
- Not now!
- Gordon!

538
00:26:25,551 --> 00:26:26,551
He's bloody vanished.

539
00:26:27,052 --> 00:26:28,452
He knows we
don't have the knife.

540
00:26:29,488 --> 00:26:30,547
We have to find Bruce.

541
00:26:32,224 --> 00:26:34,125
- Donna Falcone.
- Do not call me that.

542
00:26:34,226 --> 00:26:35,990
- You gotta hear us out.
- No, I don't.

543
00:26:36,395 --> 00:26:38,830
I didn't invite you here.
We're not having this meeting.

544
00:26:39,064 --> 00:26:41,932
Now I'm gonna ask you,
please, politely, to leave.

545
00:26:42,401 --> 00:26:43,699
We served your father for years.

546
00:26:44,036 --> 00:26:45,956
When Penguin took over,
we had to go underground.

547
00:26:46,738 --> 00:26:48,707
Then we heard a Falcone
was back in Gotham,

548
00:26:49,374 --> 00:26:50,374
we knew it was time.

549
00:26:50,809 --> 00:26:53,040
Did you ignore my father
like you're ignoring me?

550
00:26:53,579 --> 00:26:54,877
I just told you to leave.

551
00:26:55,647 --> 00:26:57,258
Just tell us when your
father's coming back

552
00:26:57,282 --> 00:26:58,614
to put that Penguin
in his place.

553
00:26:58,784 --> 00:27:01,652
Please understand me,
my father is not coming back.

554
00:27:01,820 --> 00:27:02,947
This is Penguin's city.

555
00:27:03,055 --> 00:27:04,079
And I want you to leave

556
00:27:04,156 --> 00:27:06,887
- before he finds out you're here...
- Too late, I'm afraid.

557
00:27:09,061 --> 00:27:10,501
Victor, how many
graves did you dig?

558
00:27:11,330 --> 00:27:12,330
Two.

559
00:27:12,898 --> 00:27:13,898
But they're roomy.

560
00:27:20,639 --> 00:27:21,639
(GUNSHOTS)

561
00:27:33,385 --> 00:27:34,385
And there we are.

562
00:27:34,720 --> 00:27:36,518
My paranoia is put to rest.

563
00:27:36,955 --> 00:27:38,856
Phew! (SNIGGERING)

564
00:27:40,225 --> 00:27:41,284
You used me.

565
00:27:41,793 --> 00:27:42,988
(BREATHING HEAVILY)

566
00:27:46,365 --> 00:27:47,697
You had me come to your club.

567
00:27:48,700 --> 00:27:49,777
You knew word would get out.

568
00:27:49,801 --> 00:27:51,633
That my father's
loyalists would find me.

569
00:27:52,204 --> 00:27:53,672
I was a worm on your hook.

570
00:27:57,176 --> 00:27:59,145
With one difference, my dear.

571
00:27:59,645 --> 00:28:01,136
The worm is usually eaten.

572
00:28:02,514 --> 00:28:03,514
So you trust me now?

573
00:28:04,116 --> 00:28:05,209
You're alive, aren't you?

574
00:28:09,121 --> 00:28:11,716
I would plant roses.
They cover up the smell.

575
00:28:14,860 --> 00:28:16,556
My father wouldn't
have killed those men.

576
00:28:19,164 --> 00:28:22,259
He would've invited me to his club,
as you did, but then gone further.

577
00:28:22,734 --> 00:28:24,225
He would've invited
me to dinners,

578
00:28:24,770 --> 00:28:25,897
had us seen in public,

579
00:28:26,605 --> 00:28:28,767
sent the message that
the old order supports me.

580
00:28:30,609 --> 00:28:33,545
And those men you killed
would've pledged you loyalty.

581
00:28:36,048 --> 00:28:37,209
Now they're fertilizer.

582
00:28:38,217 --> 00:28:40,743
See, my father, he knew how
to build on the strength of others.

583
00:28:41,887 --> 00:28:42,887
(SCOFFS)

584
00:28:43,689 --> 00:28:44,689
But it's your city.

585
00:28:53,098 --> 00:28:54,896
New times, new methods.

586
00:29:09,214 --> 00:29:11,054
There was an ancient
Sumerian jewelry box here.

587
00:29:11,416 --> 00:29:12,416
I just replaced it.

588
00:29:12,951 --> 00:29:14,010
Hiding in plain sight.

589
00:29:15,420 --> 00:29:16,420
Good thinking.

590
00:29:18,090 --> 00:29:22,585
Bruce, there's a
legend about this knife.

591
00:29:23,895 --> 00:29:26,763
(SIGHS) That terrible
things happen because of it.

592
00:29:27,733 --> 00:29:30,430
But what if it's
not just a legend?

593
00:29:31,603 --> 00:29:33,003
My grandfather is dead.

594
00:29:34,473 --> 00:29:35,805
The man that killed him,

595
00:29:36,742 --> 00:29:38,574
he can't have this.

596
00:29:38,844 --> 00:29:39,844
I won't let him.

597
00:29:41,280 --> 00:29:42,280
I promise.

598
00:29:42,347 --> 00:29:43,747
(GLASS BREAKING)

599
00:29:44,583 --> 00:29:46,313
We have to get out
of here. Run, run!

600
00:29:49,087 --> 00:29:50,087
(SNIFFING AND SCREECHING)

601
00:30:30,228 --> 00:30:31,696
(SNIFFING FRANTICALLY)

602
00:30:37,002 --> 00:30:39,335
The Nanda Parbat consulate
is giving us the run around

603
00:30:39,404 --> 00:30:41,168
and we can't get in
there without a warrant.

604
00:30:41,506 --> 00:30:42,986
- Bruce still hasn't called you?
- No.

605
00:30:43,909 --> 00:30:45,486
What the bloody hell were
you doing letting him tag along

606
00:30:45,510 --> 00:30:47,638
with some murder investigation
in the first place, eh?

607
00:30:47,846 --> 00:30:51,544
No, you do not get to lecture me
about a lack of honesty and openness.

608
00:30:51,650 --> 00:30:53,778
You and Bruce have been
up to something for weeks.

609
00:30:54,820 --> 00:30:56,448
What have you two
gotten yourselves into?

610
00:30:58,290 --> 00:30:59,724
Damn it, Alfred.
I'm on your side.

611
00:30:59,825 --> 00:31:01,316
Well, that may be, mate.

612
00:31:01,393 --> 00:31:03,953
But whatever Master Bruce
decides to tell you is his decision.

613
00:31:04,196 --> 00:31:05,196
Isn't it?

614
00:31:06,231 --> 00:31:07,231
You are his guardian.

615
00:31:07,332 --> 00:31:09,164
You are allowed
to violate his privacy

616
00:31:09,234 --> 00:31:10,545
if it is gonna keep
him out of danger.

617
00:31:10,569 --> 00:31:11,569
Stick to the point.

618
00:31:12,371 --> 00:31:14,465
What are you doing
to find my boy?

619
00:31:15,173 --> 00:31:16,402
What can I do, huh?

620
00:31:17,075 --> 00:31:19,203
I have every cop
in this city on alert.

621
00:31:19,378 --> 00:31:21,506
But as long as Ra's al
Ghul thinks he has that knife,

622
00:31:21,880 --> 00:31:22,973
his life is in danger.

623
00:31:23,048 --> 00:31:24,258
Yeah, right, and what
about the other boy?

624
00:31:24,282 --> 00:31:25,493
Did he tell you where
he put the knife?

625
00:31:25,517 --> 00:31:27,918
No. But I have to assume
Bruce is taking him to get it.

626
00:31:29,121 --> 00:31:30,121
All right.

627
00:31:32,691 --> 00:31:34,683
There's gotta be a clue
or something inside here.

628
00:31:38,196 --> 00:31:39,687
- What?
- You stay here.

629
00:31:39,765 --> 00:31:40,765
What?

630
00:31:41,433 --> 00:31:43,425
The access I gave
you depended on trust.

631
00:31:43,735 --> 00:31:46,227
If you can't be honest with
me, there's nothing I can do.

632
00:31:47,272 --> 00:31:48,272
Harper.

633
00:31:49,641 --> 00:31:52,270
If Mr. Pennyworth
tries to leave, arrest him.

634
00:31:53,111 --> 00:31:54,111
On what charge?

635
00:31:54,446 --> 00:31:55,505
She'll think of something.

636
00:31:57,349 --> 00:31:58,349
(FOOTSTEPS APPROACHING)

637
00:32:06,925 --> 00:32:07,925
(GASPING AND SCREECHING)

638
00:32:13,965 --> 00:32:15,058
(SNIFFING)

639
00:32:20,806 --> 00:32:22,104
I went to the pier.

640
00:32:24,242 --> 00:32:26,438
I waited. Ed didn't show.

641
00:32:28,113 --> 00:32:29,113
He...

642
00:32:30,148 --> 00:32:32,777
- God, just get on with it!
- Right.

643
00:32:35,020 --> 00:32:36,020
One, two...

644
00:32:36,588 --> 00:32:39,183
(SINGING) <i>I waited and
waited but you never came</i>

645
00:32:39,424 --> 00:32:41,586
<i>You must be a coward
or worse, a lamebrain</i>

646
00:32:41,860 --> 00:32:44,125
<i>I 'Il give you one
chance to make it right</i>

647
00:32:44,496 --> 00:32:46,590
<i>Meet me again,
this time at midnight</i>

648
00:32:46,798 --> 00:32:48,096
<i>I 'Il make this one easy</i>

649
00:32:48,166 --> 00:32:50,260
<i>This place makes some people lie</i>

650
00:32:50,335 --> 00:32:52,770
<i>Some people speak
and some people cry</i>

651
00:32:57,843 --> 00:32:59,053
Honestly, that
could be anything.

652
00:32:59,077 --> 00:33:00,568
Can we please just
torture them, now?

653
00:33:00,645 --> 00:33:01,645
Why not?

654
00:33:03,648 --> 00:33:04,648
Wait a minute.

655
00:33:06,418 --> 00:33:07,418
(EXHALES)

656
00:33:09,955 --> 00:33:10,955
(SCREECHING)

657
00:33:28,840 --> 00:33:29,840
Run.

658
00:33:38,984 --> 00:33:39,984
Bruce.

659
00:33:41,753 --> 00:33:42,753
Down!

660
00:33:47,058 --> 00:33:48,058
(BRUCE GASPING)

661
00:33:50,028 --> 00:33:51,656
Let's go. Let's go.

662
00:33:58,603 --> 00:34:00,162
Take him, take him, go.

663
00:34:04,242 --> 00:34:05,242
Get off of me.

664
00:34:06,745 --> 00:34:07,745
(GRUNTING)

665
00:34:23,728 --> 00:34:24,728
(COUGHING)

666
00:34:54,626 --> 00:34:55,719
(GRUNTING)

667
00:35:04,369 --> 00:35:05,894
It's time you tell
me what's going on.

668
00:35:06,471 --> 00:35:08,064
What is so important
about this knife?

669
00:35:08,373 --> 00:35:09,864
Why are people dying for it?

670
00:35:10,542 --> 00:35:12,101
Excellent question, Detective.

671
00:35:12,577 --> 00:35:14,739
And you shall have
your answer soon.

672
00:35:15,647 --> 00:35:18,708
Just give me the knife
and I will release the boy.

673
00:35:19,050 --> 00:35:20,250
You'd like that, wouldn't you?

674
00:35:22,053 --> 00:35:23,053
(SOBBING)

675
00:35:24,556 --> 00:35:25,556
Of course.

676
00:35:26,591 --> 00:35:27,591
Bruce, hand me the knife.

677
00:35:32,464 --> 00:35:34,626
Bruce. Hand me the knife.

678
00:35:34,799 --> 00:35:35,858
You don't understand.

679
00:35:37,469 --> 00:35:38,596
He killed Alfred.

680
00:35:39,604 --> 00:35:40,964
And then brought
him back to life.

681
00:35:41,106 --> 00:35:43,132
And Alex's grandfather
was afraid of this knife.

682
00:35:44,976 --> 00:35:46,137
I can't give it to him.

683
00:35:47,545 --> 00:35:49,605
If we don't hand it
over now, Alex is dead.

684
00:35:49,948 --> 00:35:51,228
- That is all that matters.
- No.

685
00:35:52,384 --> 00:35:53,477
You don't know who he is.

686
00:35:53,985 --> 00:35:54,985
I can't.

687
00:35:56,955 --> 00:35:58,514
Well done, Bruce.

688
00:35:59,524 --> 00:36:01,789
You're finally beginning
to see things clearly.

689
00:36:03,194 --> 00:36:04,194
The question is...

690
00:36:05,797 --> 00:36:07,163
(KNIFE SLICING)

691
00:36:07,699 --> 00:36:08,758
Are you strong enough?

692
00:36:11,469 --> 00:36:12,469
(SCREAMS)

693
00:36:13,138 --> 00:36:14,367
- No.
- Drop the weapon.

694
00:36:14,706 --> 00:36:16,004
Hands in the air, on your knees.

695
00:36:18,276 --> 00:36:19,276
Arrest me.

696
00:36:27,719 --> 00:36:29,551
Ra's al Ghul is on
his way to Blackgate.

697
00:36:30,155 --> 00:36:32,056
And we made contact
with Alex's parents.

698
00:36:32,223 --> 00:36:34,063
It's gonna take them a
couple days to get home.

699
00:36:36,561 --> 00:36:37,561
Bruce.

700
00:36:40,331 --> 00:36:41,822
Ra's is a psychopath.

701
00:36:42,767 --> 00:36:44,599
You're not responsible
for his actions.

702
00:36:47,072 --> 00:36:48,072
I wanna help.

703
00:36:48,273 --> 00:36:49,866
But everything
you've said so far,

704
00:36:50,275 --> 00:36:53,211
Alfred dying and coming
back to life, magical waters.

705
00:36:53,878 --> 00:36:55,346
Give me something I can believe.

706
00:36:56,347 --> 00:36:58,111
Perhaps now you
understand why we held back.

707
00:36:59,184 --> 00:37:01,119
It doesn't matter what
you say, Detective.

708
00:37:04,556 --> 00:37:05,683
This is all my fault.

709
00:37:09,194 --> 00:37:10,992
Alex is dead because of me.

710
00:37:12,864 --> 00:37:13,864
I killed him.

711
00:37:23,842 --> 00:37:24,842
Oswald!

712
00:37:27,579 --> 00:37:28,579
I waited for you.

713
00:37:29,914 --> 00:37:30,914
Again.

714
00:37:32,183 --> 00:37:34,175
And you didn't show. Again.

715
00:37:36,087 --> 00:37:37,087
You coward.

716
00:37:40,458 --> 00:37:41,458
Where are you?

717
00:37:42,427 --> 00:37:43,427
Here.

718
00:37:43,762 --> 00:37:45,492
And I may be many things,

719
00:37:45,597 --> 00:37:46,656
but I'm not a coward.

720
00:37:49,567 --> 00:37:50,967
Your riddles suck.

721
00:37:51,836 --> 00:37:54,601
"This place may
make some people lie,

722
00:37:55,240 --> 00:37:57,766
"some people speak,
and some people cry."

723
00:37:59,077 --> 00:38:00,272
That could mean anything.

724
00:38:00,445 --> 00:38:02,004
Lying, speaking, crying.

725
00:38:02,647 --> 00:38:05,173
You're just describing a
range of human behavior.

726
00:38:05,450 --> 00:38:06,450
No!

727
00:38:06,684 --> 00:38:09,813
I meant, lying, as
in "lying dead..."

728
00:38:10,822 --> 00:38:12,381
Laying down. Lying down.

729
00:38:12,924 --> 00:38:13,924
As in dead.

730
00:38:14,092 --> 00:38:15,092
Oh.

731
00:38:15,393 --> 00:38:18,488
The answer was Stoker
Cemetery. Clearly.

732
00:38:19,597 --> 00:38:21,088
Only an idiot wouldn't see that.

733
00:38:21,900 --> 00:38:25,132
Face it, Ed. You can't make
riddles to save your ass anymore.

734
00:38:26,971 --> 00:38:28,269
I guess she was right.

735
00:38:28,339 --> 00:38:30,672
- What? Who was right?
- Your number one fan.

736
00:38:31,242 --> 00:38:33,177
She said that there was
something wrong with you.

737
00:38:35,146 --> 00:38:36,774
Took me a while
to believe it, but...

738
00:38:37,315 --> 00:38:38,315
(CHUCKLING)

739
00:38:39,684 --> 00:38:40,845
You're not smart.

740
00:38:41,019 --> 00:38:42,180
(LAUGHS)

741
00:38:42,287 --> 00:38:43,287
I am too smart.

742
00:38:44,489 --> 00:38:46,958
- I'm the Riddler.
- Oh, God, that name again?

743
00:38:47,158 --> 00:38:49,678
Tell me, how long did it take
you to come up with those riddles?

744
00:38:50,161 --> 00:38:52,221
- I don't know.
- A minute? Two minutes?

745
00:38:53,398 --> 00:38:54,398
A few hours.

746
00:38:55,400 --> 00:38:56,400
Six hours.

747
00:38:58,303 --> 00:38:59,362
- Six hours?
- Yes!

748
00:38:59,804 --> 00:39:01,033
For two bad riddles?

749
00:39:01,673 --> 00:39:03,574
Does that sound
anything like the old you?

750
00:39:04,843 --> 00:39:08,678
Well, perhaps I'm still recovering
from being frozen in a block of ice!

751
00:39:10,415 --> 00:39:12,646
But revenge will certainly
make me feel better.

752
00:39:12,817 --> 00:39:14,161
You will never
have revenge on me.

753
00:39:14,185 --> 00:39:15,881
Ah! Yeah, I will.

754
00:39:16,321 --> 00:39:17,949
- No, you won't.
- Oswald.

755
00:39:18,823 --> 00:39:19,823
I'm gonna shoot you.

756
00:39:22,727 --> 00:39:26,459
Yes. You might
shoot me, kill me,

757
00:39:28,166 --> 00:39:30,533
but only he can
get revenge on me.

758
00:39:32,003 --> 00:39:33,403
The man I froze on that pier.

759
00:39:34,873 --> 00:39:39,140
The man who would've killed himself
before writing those idiotic riddles.

760
00:39:39,811 --> 00:39:42,144
The man whose name
I still will not speak.

761
00:39:44,682 --> 00:39:45,682
But he's gone.

762
00:39:45,917 --> 00:39:46,917
(EXHALES)

763
00:39:47,585 --> 00:39:48,585
You're just Ed Nygma.

764
00:39:49,554 --> 00:39:51,113
And, not even Ed Nygma,

765
00:39:52,123 --> 00:39:53,921
because Ed Nygma
would've noticed something.

766
00:39:56,127 --> 00:39:57,127
What?

767
00:39:59,564 --> 00:40:01,084
Doesn't it feel kind
of chilly in here?

768
00:40:02,800 --> 00:40:03,800
(GROANS)

769
00:40:06,437 --> 00:40:07,437
You remember him?

770
00:40:08,539 --> 00:40:09,871
My other Victor?

771
00:40:11,376 --> 00:40:13,470
I'm going to put
you on ice, again.

772
00:40:15,246 --> 00:40:16,942
You want the same
pose or something else?

773
00:40:17,048 --> 00:40:18,414
No, wait, please.

774
00:40:18,549 --> 00:40:20,245
- Got it, same pose.
- No!

775
00:40:24,088 --> 00:40:25,088
Okay, Oswald.

776
00:40:28,626 --> 00:40:29,719
I'm not the Riddler.

777
00:40:35,266 --> 00:40:36,266
Do it.

778
00:40:37,735 --> 00:40:38,735
Stop.

779
00:40:40,872 --> 00:40:41,872
I've changed my mind.

780
00:40:44,642 --> 00:40:45,642
I'm not gonna freeze you.

781
00:40:46,911 --> 00:40:48,573
I would only be
freezing Ed Nygma.

782
00:40:49,314 --> 00:40:50,314
Who cares about that?

783
00:40:51,582 --> 00:40:54,746
No. A better revenge
is having you live,

784
00:40:55,787 --> 00:40:57,153
knowing that you're not him

785
00:40:58,323 --> 00:40:59,484
and you never will be again.

786
00:41:02,126 --> 00:41:03,126
Goodbye, Ed.

787
00:41:12,637 --> 00:41:13,798
Who am I?

788
00:41:16,274 --> 00:41:18,334
I must have been seven or eight.

789
00:41:20,678 --> 00:41:21,678
It was late.

790
00:41:23,147 --> 00:41:25,116
I snuck downstairs
to find my father

791
00:41:25,183 --> 00:41:27,345
with a man before
him on his knees.

792
00:41:28,086 --> 00:41:29,418
Begging for forgiveness.

793
00:41:30,922 --> 00:41:32,914
My father stabbed
him in the neck.

794
00:41:34,625 --> 00:41:37,424
The next morning, there
wasn't a trace of blood.

795
00:41:37,662 --> 00:41:40,154
And we had
breakfast at that table.

796
00:41:42,467 --> 00:41:45,926
Now you'd think I'd want to
stay far away from Gotham

797
00:41:46,004 --> 00:41:50,704
but this house, this
city, is in my veins.

798
00:41:52,343 --> 00:41:53,470
I'm home, Jim.

799
00:41:55,913 --> 00:41:59,145
Word on the street is three
Falcone capos have gone missing.

800
00:42:01,719 --> 00:42:02,846
They were in hiding.

801
00:42:03,721 --> 00:42:05,713
Penguin used me as
bait to draw them out.

802
00:42:06,657 --> 00:42:07,657
And killed them.

803
00:42:10,161 --> 00:42:11,161
And you let him?

804
00:42:12,530 --> 00:42:13,998
Don't act the innocent, Jim.

805
00:42:15,066 --> 00:42:17,001
You came to Carmine
Falcone for help.

806
00:42:17,068 --> 00:42:18,832
It was never going
to be bloodless.

807
00:42:20,338 --> 00:42:21,362
So is that your plan?

808
00:42:23,474 --> 00:42:26,535
Get close to Penguin, gain
his confidence, what then?

809
00:42:27,211 --> 00:42:28,555
I need to know what
you're going to do.

810
00:42:28,579 --> 00:42:30,878
No, you don't.

811
00:42:32,216 --> 00:42:33,844
You want this city
back from Penguin.

812
00:42:34,218 --> 00:42:35,311
I'll give that to you.

813
00:42:36,888 --> 00:42:37,981
But you have to trust me.

814
00:42:40,925 --> 00:42:41,925
(GLASS SHATTERING)

815
00:42:55,907 --> 00:42:57,034
(SIRENS WAILING)

816
00:42:59,110 --> 00:43:00,390
(INDISTINCT ANNOUNCEMENTS ON PA)

817
00:43:05,283 --> 00:43:06,283
(ELECTRIC GATES BUZZING)

818
00:43:10,822 --> 00:43:11,822
(INDISTINCT SHOUTING)

819
00:43:16,160 --> 00:43:17,600
(SINISTER INSTRUMENTAL
MUSIC PLAYING)

820
00:44:00,671 --> 00:44:01,671
English - SDH
